# Copy to .env and set both variables to the same password.
# docker-compose reads POSTGRES_PASSWORD; the CLI reads DATABASE_URL.
# Dev database only — it holds synthetic events and is bound to 127.0.0.1.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   generate a password with: openssl rand -hex 16
#
# DATABASE_URL takes the form:
#   postgres://postgres:<password>@localhost:5432/events
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
DATABASE_URL=

# Which model drives the agent (ADR-0015). Neither is needed for the test suite:
# every path is asserted against a scripted API, so `npx vitest run` needs no key.
#
# openrouter (default) — an OpenAI-shaped loop, and any of ~336 tool-capable models. Cheap
# reasoning models cost roughly a tenth of Opus on output, which is what makes
# iterating on a prompt affordable. A model that cannot make a structured tool call is
# refused by name before the run rather than producing an empty transcript after it.
#   OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-v1-…
#   ENGINE_MODEL=moonshotai/kimi-k2-thinking   # or deepseek/deepseek-r1, minimax/minimax-m2.5
#
# anthropic — the SDK's tool runner: native thinking, a maintained agentic loop, prompt
# caching. The better engine, for a run on a real repository that deserves it.
#   ENGINE_PROVIDER=anthropic
#   ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-…
#
# ENGINE_EFFORT is the single biggest lever on spend: low|medium|high|xhigh|max.
# `high` is right for a real fix; `low` answers the only question prompt work asks.
#   ENGINE_EFFORT=low

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE GITHUB APP (ADR-0012). Needed by anything that talks to GitHub: the local
# `serve`, and the hosted `plane`. NOT by a runner — that is the point of the split.
#
# Create one at https://github.com/settings/apps/new. It needs read/write on issues,
# pull requests and contents, and a webhook pointing at wherever deliveries should
# land. The private key is downloaded once; keep the file, not the copy in your
# clipboard.
#   GITHUB_APP_ID=123456
#   GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=~/.config/test-framework-v2/github-app.pem
#   GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
#
# The App's slug — the name in https://github.com/apps/<slug>. Without it the "Install
# on GitHub" button falls back to GitHub's create-an-app form, which is correct when
# you have no App and confusing when you do.
#   GITHUB_APP_SLUG=your-app-slug

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE CONTROL PLANE (milestone 9, ADR-0019). One address GitHub can always reach.
# Holds the App key, the log and the UI; holds no Docker and no model key, and
# executes nothing.
#
# OAuth is the same App: its client id and secret, from the App's settings page.
#   GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=Iv1.xxxxxxxxxxxx
#   GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=xxxxxxxx
#   ENGINE_PLANE_CALLBACK_URL=https://plane.example.com/auth/github/callback
#   ENGINE_BLOB_ROOT=/var/lib/test-framework/blobs
#   ENGINE_PLANE_INSECURE=1   # http in development; the session cookie drops Secure
#
# What seals a stored model key or repository secret (M10, ADR-0017). Required, even
# on a deployment that injects nothing: the plane accepts a model key as soon as
# anyone signs in, and it will not accept one it cannot encrypt. Lose it and every
# stored value is unreadable — which is the property, and it is why this belongs in a
# secret store rather than in a file next to the database URL.
#   PLANE_SECRETS_KEY=$(openssl rand -base64 32)
#
# The key belongs to the DATABASE, not to the deployment. A laptop and a hosted plane
# pointing at the same Postgres must carry the same one, or whichever wrote a row first
# produces ciphertext the other cannot open — and that failure arrives at run time, not
# at boot.
#
# Whether stored repository secrets are INJECTED into runs, as opposed to held. Off
# until the executor has been shown to seal a sandbox before its first turn.
#   ENGINE_SECRETS_ENABLED=1
#
#   npm run plane

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A RUNNER (milestone 9d). Your machine. Needs Docker and a model key; needs no
# inbound connection, no tunnel, and NO GitHub credential — it asks the plane for a
# token per run.
#
# The token comes from the plane: sign in, open a repository, "Runners", pair one.
# It is shown once.
#   ENGINE_PLANE_URL=https://plane.example.com
#   ENGINE_RUNNER_TOKEN=tfr_...
#   ENGINE_IMAGE=test-framework-v2-sandbox:latest
#   ENGINE_AGENT_IMAGE=test-framework-v2-agent:latest
#   ENGINE_BLOB_ROOT=./.blobs
#
#   docker build -t test-framework-v2-sandbox:latest .
#   docker build -t test-framework-v2-agent:latest -f Dockerfile.agent .
#   npm run runner

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE WORKER (milestone 10, 10e). The runner WE operate — a Node process with no
# Docker, no inbound port, and no App key, running every phase in a Firecracker
# microVM instead of a container on this machine.
#
# `ENGINE_EXECUTOR=vercel` makes the images REFERENCES the platform pulls, so the
# local tags above are meaningless to it. `npm run images:push` publishes both and
# prints these two, pinned by digest:
#   ENGINE_EXECUTOR=vercel
#   ENGINE_IMAGE=vcr.vercel.com/<team>/<project>/test-framework-v2-sandbox@sha256:...
#   ENGINE_AGENT_IMAGE=vcr.vercel.com/<team>/<project>/test-framework-v2-agent@sha256:...
#   ENGINE_VERCEL_REGION=iad1
#
# All three together or none — a partial set silently falls back to a `vercel login`
# a container does not have:
#   VERCEL_TOKEN=...        # vercel.com/account/settings/tokens
#   VERCEL_TEAM_ID=team_...
#   VERCEL_PROJECT_ID=prj_...
#
# And its runner token, from `npm run worker:pair` — which must run AFTER the plane
# has applied `db/schema.sql`, because pairing writes a null installation_id and the
# column only accepts one once the schema has run. `npm run worker:unpair` revokes it.
